WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
And I’ll never forget there was one priest that came.

2
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.300
And I might’ve been maybe about . . . I was in my twenties.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.020
And he came in and he saw how the church was.

4
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:22.980
And he said, “Wait, before I proceed with the liturgy, the homily,” he said, “No, y’all can’t have this.

5
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.580
I want you to get up, and I want y’all to come, y’all on this side,

6
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:31.527
blacks on this side, whites . . . Look, y’all need to come together for this liturgy.”

7
00:00:31.527 --> 00:00:35.980
And I think he lasted about a week. [laughs] I’m serious!

8
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.860
And after about a week or two weeks we were getting a new priest.

9
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:46.500
And then everybody was back to sitting . . . But it’s just, I mean, nobody talked about it.

10
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.560
Even the graveyard is segregated.

11
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.760
Whites on one side, blacks on the other side.

12
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.600
They built this mausoleum-type deal, but it’s still like that.

13
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:07.300
And if you go to the white side you not going to find the names of the . . . like Hawkins and like you find that’s on the manifest for the slaves.

14
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.320
If you go to the white side,

15
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.740
you not going to find those names—of the last names of the people who may or may not have gone.

16
00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:19.860
And that may have been because the name, last name were already given to the slaves by the time they got down here.

17
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.220
So there is this separateness in there.
